{
  "rationale": "The race evidence from iteration 0 is clear. The oval finished in all 20 trials (mean score 950). The s-bend and slalom both ended off track in every trial, at 17% and 12% progress. Their max_error of about 0.061 and 0.062 m sits just over the off-track limit. The limit is max(0.06, tape_half_width + body_width/4). With tape_half_width 0.0125 and body_width at most 0.18, body_width/4 is at most 0.045, so the 0.06 m floor always applies and the body cannot widen the allowed error. Off-track is therefore decided by how far the robot strays from the centre line on the tight bends, not by the body. The lateral error stays small on average (rms 0.027 m) but peaks just past the limit, so the problem is peak excursion in sharp bends, not sustained drift.\n\nThe iteration 0 layout has five sensors on a 12 mm pitch, spanning only +/-24 mm, at y = 0.05 m. When the robot is offset, the outer sensors see the tape late, so the signal responds to error late. The 12 mm pitch also means the array covers only about 36 mm of the 25 mm tape plus offset, which gives little lead.\n\nChanges for this iteration:\n- Widen the array pitch to 15 mm (x = +/-30, +/-15, 0 mm). The outer sensors reach +/-30 mm, so the robot gets earlier warning of a drift toward the edge and the signal sweeps across more range before the line is lost.\n- Move the array forward to y = 0.07 m, the upper part of the allowed range. This gives more lead time on bends, which is the main lever against the peak-excursion failure.\n- Narrow the wheel track from 0.12 to 0.10 m. Turn rate is the wheel speed difference divided by track, so a narrower track gives more yaw per unit of wheel difference, a more agile turn response for the Integrator's gains.\n- Shorten the body from 0.16 to 0.14 m and reduce mass from 0.6 to 0.5 kg. Yaw inertia scales with mass times (L squared + W squared), so this lowers inertia on both counts and speeds up turn response.\n- Keep sensor_size 0.012 (matches the tape and avoids over-averaging), wheel_radius 0.03 and body_width 0.14 unchanged, since body width cannot affect the off-track limit.\n\nExpected result: the s-bend and slalom should progress further and perhaps complete, with max_error below 0.06 m. The oval should stay fast, possibly with slightly more steering oscillation from the higher-agility geometry. The main risk is that a wider, forward array reads more background on tight bends and causes lag or jitter, which the Integrator's feedback will show.",
  "geometry": {
    "sensor_positions": [
      {"x": 0.03, "y": 0.07},
      {"x": 0.015, "y": 0.07},
      {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.07},
      {"x": -0.015, "y": 0.07},
      {"x": -0.03, "y": 0.07}
    ],
    "sensor_size": 0.012,
    "wheel_track": 0.10,
    "wheel_radius": 0.03,
    "body_width": 0.14,
    "body_length": 0.14,
    "mass": 0.5
  },
  "intent": "Change one hypothesis at a time where possible, but this iteration combines the sensing and agility changes because the s-bend and slalom both fail on peak excursion. I widened the sensor array to +/-30 mm at 15 mm pitch and moved it forward to 0.07 m for earlier detection and more lead on bends. I narrowed the wheel track to 0.10 m and reduced mass and body length to speed up turn response. I expect the s-bend and slalom to progress further, ideally to completion, with max_error below the 0.06 m limit. The oval should remain a completed run. Success is a completed s-bend or slalom; a failure that stays at the same progress would point back at the controller's gain rather than the geometry.",
  "feedback_request": {
    "measurements_requested": ["completion", "progress", "completion_time", "rms_error", "max_error", "line_loss_events", "steering_oscillation", "control_effort"],
    "questions": [
      "On the s-bend and slalom, at what progress and in which bend does the robot first exceed 0.06 m? Does the widened array give earlier signal change before that point?",
      "Do the outer sensors at +/-30 mm read a non-zero value often, or do they sit at zero? If they sit at zero, the outer spacing is too wide for the steering demand.",
      "Does the 0.10 m wheel track with 0.5 kg mass show more steering oscillation on the oval than iteration 0 did? Is the extra agility causing overshoot?",
      "Does the forward offset of 0.07 m cause visible lag on tight bends, or does it help the robot anticipate them?"
    ],
    "requested_from": "Robot Integrator"
  },
  "learning": "Iteration 0 showed that body width cannot change the off-track limit because the 0.06 m floor always applies, so geometry must reduce peak lateral error through sensing lead and agility. Next iteration, if the s-bend and slalom still fail with max_error near 0.06 m, the evidence will point to a controller gain or base_speed problem rather than geometry, and I will hold the geometry fixed and say so in the intent. If they complete, I will test whether a narrower array (for example 12 mm pitch at the forward position) or a higher mass still holds stability."
}
